            <ArticleTitle>The many faces of geriatric depression.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>540-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/YCO.0b013e328311cdae</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE OF REVIEW" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Recent studies on five aspects of geriatric depression, namely subsyndromal depression, risk factors and association with chronic pain, cardiovascular disease and cognitive impairment, are reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RECENT FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Subsyndromal depression in the elderly population is not uncommon in east Asia; the prevalence is about 8-9%. Risk factors of geriatric depression include poor health, brain injury, low folate and vitamin B12 and raised plasma homocysteine levels. Depressed elderly with chronic pain are prone to suicidal ideation. Depression is a risk factor for cardiovascular diseases and mortality in coronary heart disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The growing interest in research on geriatric depression could focus on early diagnosis to help doctors treat depression better at primary care level.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
